       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. I understand it's intentional, but 5,100 mg/day is about twice the maximum labeled dose, and I can't find a clinical reason for it. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, is about timing and doesn't explain the dose. Metformin at this level carries a real risk of serious harm, including severe GI toxicity and lactic acidosis. Titrating faster or giving more won't get the patient to glycemic control sooner.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals (2,000 mg/day)\n- A lower starting dose with a titration plan, such as 500 mg PO twice daily with meals, increased weekly\n\nIf you have a clinical reason for exceeding the labeled maximum, such as a specific protocol or a toxicology or endocrine recommendation, I'd suggest talking to pharmacy and entering the order directly. They can review it and document the rationale. Tell me which of the options above you'd like, and I'll enter it now."
      }
